description: give each Unix user of a shared prefix its own identity
 Builds on the shared system prefix: with several users on one wineserver, they
 need to be distinguishable. Upstream has no notion of a second user, in four
 connected ways, and fixing any one alone leaves the others broken.
 .
   * security_unix_uid_to_sid() is binary -- you, or Anonymous Logon -- so every
     user maps to the same SID. Now a SID per uid, keeping local_user_sid's
     shape (S-1-5-21-0-0-0-<1000+uid>), the convention Windows and Samba use.
   * Every process token comes from token_create_admin(), so there is no such
     thing as a non-admin to deny. token_create_for_uid() builds a token for the
     connecting user, dropping the Administrators group, the administrative
     privileges and the high integrity label for ordinary users. Administrators
     are root and the prefix's owner -- a placeholder for group mapping via
     winbind, but a real rule an admin can read off ls -l.
   * init_registry() loads one HKCU, for one SID, at startup -- upstream's own
     FIXME says it should come from the process token. With a SID per uid, every
     user but the first then finds their hive missing and every HKCU operation
     fails with OBJECT_NAME_NOT_FOUND. Hives are now loaded on demand, per user,
     from user-<uid>.reg. MAX_SAVE_BRANCH_INFO was exactly 3 (system, userdef,
     user), so there was no room for even one more.
   * check_object_access() returns TRUE for any object with no security
     descriptor, and objects are created without one unless a caller supplies
     it. So the registry is not under-checked so much as un-checkable: no
     descriptor written to a key can protect it. New keys now get a descriptor
     from the creating process's token, and creating a subkey checks the parent
     for KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY -- falling back to an open, since a refusal may just
     mean the key already exists and is governed by its own descriptor.
 .
 The default DACL is the arrangement Windows uses for HKLM: system and
 administrators may write, everyone else may read. Naming the creating user
 instead is the obvious first guess and is wrong -- it protects HKLM keys by
 making them invisible to every other user, which breaks HKLM as shared machine
 state. Per-user privacy comes from HKCU being a separate hive, not from this.
 .
 S2 gate: 3 of 5 clauses (two users on one prefix, shared HKLM, isolated HKCU).
 Ordinary prefixes are untouched; without the system-prefix marker every path
 behaves as before.
origin: Stained Glass OS
bug: https://github.com/Stained-Glass-OS/stained-glass/issues/2
forwarded: not-needed
 Stained Glass OS does not submit patches upstream; see ADR 0006.
